| Goal | Mechanism |
|---|---|
| **Encryption at rest** | AES-256-GCM with a key derived from the master password via PBKDF2-SHA-256 (100 000 iterations). A reader of the storage sees only a random-looking base64 blob; the only thing leaked is the plaintext length + 28 bytes of overhead. |
| **Owner isolation** | Each secret lives under the key `secret:b64(owner):b64(name)`. `get` looks up exactly that key, so a lookup for owner A can never return owner B's data. |
| **Tamper detection** | The storage key (i.e. the `(owner, name)` pair) is fed as the GCM **additional authenticated data**. Any modification of the stored bytes—bit-flipping, truncation, or copying another secret's ciphertext over this key—causes the GCM authentication tag to fail, so `crypto.subtle.decrypt` throws and `get` re-throws an `Error`. |

A store opened with a **different** master password derives a different AES key; every `get` will hit the GCM tag mismatch and throw, satisfying the "must not be able to read existing secrets" requirement without needing `createSecretStore` to reject.

